  "full_text": "**\nWait, is that correct? Let me think again.\n\nOkay, so I have a classic problem here: A bat and a ball cost one dollar and ten cents in total. The bat costs one dollar more than the ball. How much does the ball cost?\n\nHmm, at first glance, I might have thought it's a straightforward arithmetic problem. If the total is $1.10 and the bat is $1 more than the ball, then I can set up some equations.\n\nLet me denote the cost of the ball as \\( b \\) dollars. Then, the cost of the bat would be \\( b + 1 \\) dollars. The total cost is the sum of the ball and the bat, so:\n\n\\( b + (b + 1) = 1.10 \\)\n\nSimplify that:\n\n\\( 2b + 1 = 1.10 \\)\n\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\n\\( 2b = 0.10 \\)\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n\\( b = 0.05 \\)\n\nSo, the ball costs 5 cents. Then the bat would cost 6 cents, and 5 + 6 = 11, which"
